WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.780
What's up? Welcome to Models and Agents Episode 50 on May 16th, 2026.

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:09.740
Let's see what shipped. Lots of news to walk through. Let's start with what actually matters.

00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:16.260
An agent that manages another agent just moved from research demo to production reality at Fin.

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:23.140
Fin, the company formerly known as Intercom, launched Fin operator. This new AI system exists

00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:29.900
only to configure, debug, and monitor the customer facing Fin agent. It pulls conversation metrics

00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:36.320
into charts, ingest product PDFs to refresh health articles, traces failed conversations back to

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:43.120
specific guidance rules, and surfaces proposed fixes as reviewable diffs. Operator runs on

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:49.060
cloud instead of Fin's own Apex models because the work resembles softer engineering more than customer

00:00:49.060 --> 00:00:56.320
support. Every change still needs explicit human approval before it goes live. Early beta users say

00:00:56.320 --> 00:01:01.720
it feels like adding five people to the operations team. General availability is set for summer

00:01:01.720 --> 00:01:08.460
2026 inside the Pro tier with usage based billing. The launch shows that enterprises will production

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:14.360
eyes meta agents when strict human oversight stays in place. Now let's move to model updates.

00:01:14.860 --> 00:01:26.220
On terminal bench 2.0 the twin 3.6-35b-a3b model with the little coder scaffold scored 24.6%.

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:34.080
That result beats Gemini 2.5 pro on the Gemini command line interface. The smaller 9B variant

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:41.660
reached 9.2%, showing that models under 10 billion parameters can now post measurable scores on hard

00:01:41.660 --> 00:01:48.240
agentic benchmarks. The leaderboard entry also highlights that scaffold choice still matters more than

00:01:48.370 --> 00:01:56.470
raw parameter count. Ziffer released the ZAYA1-8B diffusion preview. The team converted an

00:01:56.470 --> 00:02:02.870
auto regressive mixture of experts model into a discrete diffusion model. The change moved decoding

00:02:02.870 --> 00:02:09.750
from memory bandwidth bound to compute bound and delivered up to 7.7 times faster inference.

00:02:10.570 --> 00:02:16.090
Early checks show no systematic loss on standard evaluations. This marks the first public

00:02:16.090 --> 00:02:22.390
demonstration of turning an existing mixture of experts LLM into a diffusion model at this scale.

00:02:23.370 --> 00:02:30.930
Gemma 4.26b now runs an MLX with a custom turboquant back end and rotating KV cache.

00:02:31.450 --> 00:02:37.830
On an M5 MacBook Air the setup handles 128K context and 4 concurrent batches.

00:02:37.830 --> 00:02:44.810
At 8K context, it outperforms lama.cpp on both prompt processing and generation speed,

00:02:45.130 --> 00:02:50.070
while using less runtime memory. A custom kernel for sliding window attention layers

00:02:50.070 --> 00:02:53.650
preserves the two bit memory savings even at higher batch sizes.

00:02:54.510 --> 00:03:00.270
Allen AI released the MOLMO Act 2 series of 5B vision language action models.

00:03:00.990 --> 00:03:09.790
The models were fine-tuned on robotics data sets including L-I-B-E-R-O-D-R-O-I-D and Bimanual tasks.

00:03:10.370 --> 00:03:16.390
All weights, training data sets, code, and papers are fully open. They target absolute joint

00:03:16.390 --> 00:03:21.910
pose control and interactive robotic scenarios. Let's look at agent and tool developments.

00:03:22.470 --> 00:03:28.710
Recursive MAS lets agents collaborate entirely in latent space instead of exchanging text tokens.

00:03:29.350 --> 00:03:35.650
The approach yields 1.2 to 2.4 times faster inference and up to 75% token reduction.

00:03:36.230 --> 00:03:43.810
Only lightweight recursive link modules 0.31% of total parameters are trained while base models

00:03:43.810 --> 00:03:50.350
stay frozen. Tests across code, math, and medical reasoning benchmarks used open weight models.

00:03:50.830 --> 00:03:56.130
A new tutorial shows how to build a fully functional MCP-style routed agent system.

00:03:56.850 --> 00:04:01.990
The system includes dynamic tool discovery, structured planning, and context injection.

00:04:02.490 --> 00:04:07.150
It ships with a modular tool server that exposes web search, local retrieval,

00:04:07.630 --> 00:04:13.770
data set loading, and Python execution. The code is built to be extended for custom agent workflows.

00:04:14.610 --> 00:04:18.510
Nexidian is an open-source hierarchical markdown note-taking app.

00:04:19.110 --> 00:04:25.030
An autonomous background agent can reorganize notes, summarize subtrees, or extract action items.

00:04:25.670 --> 00:04:31.470
All work runs locally against open AI compatible endpoints and includes built-in version control

00:04:31.470 --> 00:04:38.230
so every AI edit can be reverted. A Docker Compose setup spins up the full stack including the task

00:04:38.230 --> 00:04:45.790
runner. On the practical side, a developer indexed roughly 200K documents on an ASUS Zenbook with

00:04:45.790 --> 00:04:53.750
Snapdragon X2 Elite Extreme. The setup uses VEC MLs on device AI database, keeps most data on

00:04:53.750 --> 00:05:02.490
disk, and maintains a small active buffer. The NPU delivers about 50% of an RTX 5060s embedding speed,

00:05:03.130 --> 00:05:09.130
inside far tighter power and thermal limits. The little coder scaffold that powered the top terminal

00:05:09.130 --> 00:05:17.110
Bench 2.0 twin entry is now public on GitHub. It shows that a relatively simple scaffold can lift a 35B

00:05:17.110 --> 00:05:22.770
mixture of experts model above larger closed models on agentic coding tasks.

00:05:23.470 --> 00:05:28.910
The repo includes the exact configuration used for the 24.6% leaderboard run.

00:05:29.470 --> 00:05:34.370
If you want to try something concrete this week, spin up the little coder scaffold against

00:05:34.370 --> 00:05:45.730
twin 3.6-35B-A3B on terminal-bench 2.0. Test recursive MAS on a small code generation or medical

00:05:45.730 --> 00:05:52.550
reasoning benchmark if you already run multiple open-weight models in sequence. Run the MCP style

00:05:52.550 --> 00:05:58.490
routed agent tutorial locally and swap in your own tool server to measure planning overhead versus

00:05:58.490 --> 00:06:05.650
a simple react loop. Clone Nexidion and pointed at a local Chuan or Gemma endpoint to experiment

00:06:05.650 --> 00:06:13.430
with autonomous note reorganization under version control. On the horizon, fin operator moves from

00:06:13.430 --> 00:06:19.850
early access beta to general availability this summer inside the Pro tier. Zifra plans to release

00:06:19.850 --> 00:06:26.210
trained weights and quality benchmarks for the ZAYA1 diffusion model once training completes.

00:06:27.010 --> 00:06:33.190
Allen AI continues releasing new MOLMO Act 2 fine tunes on additional robotics datasets

00:06:33.190 --> 00:06:39.090
with full training code and data. Okay, let's pop the hood on diffusion decoding for LLMs.

00:06:39.750 --> 00:06:44.930
The technique replaces sequential token by token sampling with iterative denoising of a full

00:06:44.930 --> 00:06:51.410
latent sequence. The model starts with random noise and applies a learned denoiser for a fixed number

00:06:51.410 --> 00:06:58.230
of steps, refining every position simultaneously. Expensive operations become matrix multiplies

00:06:58.230 --> 00:07:05.070
instead of memory bound KV cache reads, which is why Zifra saw 7.7 times speed ups on the 8B

00:07:05.070 --> 00:07:11.070
mixture of experts model. Training adds a second loss term and requires careful scheduling of noise

00:07:11.070 --> 00:07:17.310
levels across diffusion steps. Quality matches autoregressive baselines only when the schedule is

00:07:17.310 --> 00:07:23.010
tuned per model size. Below roughly 7B parameters, the gap widens quickly.

00:07:23.750 --> 00:07:29.290
When you need maximum tokens per second on short to medium outputs and can accept a small quality

00:07:29.290 --> 00:07:35.550
regression during early training, diffusion decoding is worth trying. For long coherent generation

00:07:35.550 --> 00:07:42.250
or when you must stay inside existing autoregressive fine tunes, the classic next token approach remains

00:07:42.250 --> 00:07:49.050
simpler. The common gotcha is assuming any diffusion schedule works. Without per model calibration,

00:07:49.330 --> 00:07:55.750
the output often collapses into repetitive patterns after step 4. Tomorrow, keep an eye on Zifra's

00:07:55.750 --> 00:08:03.190
release of the trained ZyA1 weights and the first full quality benchmarks. That's models and agents

00:08:03.190 --> 00:08:08.190
for today. If you found this useful, share it with someone who's trying to keep up with all these

00:08:08.190 --> 00:08:14.830
changes and subscribe so you don't miss tomorrow's update. The AI world moves fast. We'll help you

00:08:14.830 --> 00:08:22.410
keep up. See you tomorrow. This episode used AI voice synthesis of my voice. Editorial selection and

00:08:22.410 --> 00:08:23.790
analysis are my own.
